.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt STATFS 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm statfs ,
.Nm fstatfs ,
.Nm statfs64 ,
.Nm fstatfs64
.Nd get file-system statistics
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/statfs.h
.Ft int
.Fn statfs "const char *path" "struct statfs *buf"
.Ft int
.Fn fstatfs "int fd" "struct statfs *buf"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn statfs
reports the file system containing
.Fa path
in
.Fa buf .
On an ordinary file system the path must name an existing object and is
resolved with symbolic links followed; a synthetic path is not checked
for existence, so
.Pa /dev/nonexistent
answers devfs's numbers and
.Pa /proc/PID/fd/N
proc's, without following the descriptor.
.Fn fstatfs
selects the file system by an open descriptor.
.Pp
The result fields are:
.Bl -tag -width "f_files, f_ffree"
.It Va f_type
The file-system magic number.
.It Va f_bsize
The byte unit of the block counts.
.It Va f_blocks
The total count in those units.
.It Va f_bfree
The free count in those units.
.It Va f_bavail
The free count an unprivileged caller may use: on ext2,
.Va f_bfree
less the blocks the superblock reserves for root, and elsewhere equal to
.Va f_bfree .
.It Va f_files , f_ffree
The inodes and the free inodes on ext2; zero on the other types, which keep
no fixed count of them.
.It Va f_fsid
Zero.
.It Va f_namelen
255, the reported maximum name length in bytes.
.It Va f_frsize
Equal to
.Va f_bsize .
.It Va f_flags
Zero, including on read-only mounts.
.El
.Pp
Reserved fields are zero.
The file-system values are:
.Bl -tag -width "Mounted KRFFS"
.It KRFFS boot root
Magic 0x4b524646.
The block unit is 1024 bytes.
Total space is the image region's size divided by 1024;
free space is the free nodes' usable bytes divided by 1024.
.It Mounted KRFFS
Magic 0x4b52.
Total and free space are the mounted volume's corresponding byte counts
divided by 1024, with a block unit of 1024.
.It ext2
Magic 0xef53.
The block unit is the volume's block size.
Total and free counts are the volume's block counts.
An ext2 root uses these values too.
.It FAT32
Magic 0x4d44.
The block unit is the cluster size, and the counts are total and free
data clusters.
Free clusters are counted when needed and the count is maintained
as allocation changes.
.It exFAT
Magic 0x2011bab0.
The block unit is the cluster size, and the counts are total and free
clusters.
Free space is counted from the allocation bitmap when needed and the
count is maintained as allocation changes.
.It Pa /proc
Magic 0x9fa0, block unit 1024, and zero block counts.
.It Pa /dev
Magic 0x1373, block unit 1024, and zero block counts.
.El
.Pp
For
.Fn fstatfs ,
proc-file descriptors report the proc values.
Device, console, pseudo-terminal master, and synthetic directory
descriptors report the devfs values.
This includes synthetic directory descriptors under
.Pa /proc .
A descriptor opened with
.Dv O_PATH
on a
.Pa /dev
node reports the devfs values, and one on a
.Pa /proc
node that is not a directory the proc values; one on a
.Pa /proc
directory is a synthetic directory descriptor and reports the devfs
values, as above.
Ordinary file and directory descriptors report their mount's values,
those opened with
.Dv O_PATH
included.
Other descriptor types, including pipes, sockets, and pseudo-terminal
slaves, return
.Er ENOSYS .
.Ss System call interface
The i386 kernel calls are
.Nm statfs64
(268) and
.Nm fstatfs64
(269).
EBX is the path pointer or descriptor, ECX is the result structure size,
and EDX is the result pointer.
The size must be 84 bytes.
.Pp
The kernel structure contains 32-bit
.Va f_type
and
.Va f_bsize ;
five 64-bit counts
.Va f_blocks , f_bfree , f_bavail , f_files ,
and
.Va f_ffree ;
two 32-bit
.Va f_fsid
words; 32-bit
.Va f_namelen , f_frsize ,
and
.Va f_flags ;
and four reserved 32-bit words.
.Pp
Musl's
.Fn statfs
and
.Fn fstatfs
issue these calls and supply the size.
With
.Dv _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE ,
.Nm statfs64
and
.Nm fstatfs64
are macros for these functions and have no separate prototypes.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel calls return negative error numbers on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/vfsprobe.c
calls
.Nm statfs64
with the result structure size set to 84 bytes and checks the distinct
magic numbers
for the KRFFS or ext2 root and
.Pa /proc .
The expected root magic comes from
.Pa /proc/mounts .
The ext2 and selfbuild-ext2 suites also run this probe on an ext2 root.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
Search permission on a path component was denied.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Path resolution entered a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A path or result pointer is null or cannot be accessed as required,
or there is no current process.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The kernel result size is not 84 bytes, or the selected file system
has no statistics operation.
.It Bq Er EIO
File-system I/O or metadata validation failed, or a mount's device
was removed.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A copied path, component, or symbolic-link expansion exceeds its buffer.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The path is empty or, on an ordinary file system, a required component
does not exist.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
A node needed for path resolution cannot be allocated.
.It Bq Er ENOSYS
The descriptor type has no file-system statistics.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A path component is not a directory, or a path that ends in a slash or
in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
reaches something else, as on Linux.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mount 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
